WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.000
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, there was a thread running

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.000
through the whole event that nobody had quite planned.

3
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:11.120
What happens to review and to trust when generating a change

4
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:14.640
costs 30 seconds and reading it costs an hour.

5
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.160
Let's first hear from Jack Wotherspoon at Google, who works on Antigravity

6
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.480
CLI, on the drive-by pull request and why he thinks

7
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.240
the social contract of open source is coming apart.

8
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.160
So here's an example. I work for Google as a developer advocate.

9
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.560
I've been in open source for the past five years at Google, working on Google

10
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.920
ADK, so Agent Development Kit, and most recently Gemini CLI.

11
00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.680
So what are the main issues?

12
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.320
Breaking down the barriers with AI should be a win, right?

13
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.920
Makes it easier for people to contribute to these projects.

14
00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.480
But what's the flip side?

15
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.720
And this is one that I've seen a lot, and it's the drive-by PR, right?

16
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.760
So you have, you're using a library or a framework or whatever

17
00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.080
it may be, and something goes wrong.

18
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.080
You say, oh, I've got the error message.

19
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.760
Let me just go and fix this.

20
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.640
You know, I know it's an open source project.

21
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:06.640
Let me go and contribute to this.

22
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.840
So you just throw in your stack trace,

23
00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.920
you clone the repository and ask your agent to fix it.

24
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.000
So that takes like 30 seconds on your end.

25
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.200
But the flip side, and we'll assume that this is actually good code generated.

26
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.840
We'll assume that for this scenario.

27
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.600
Then you've got the maintainer who actually has to go and review that.

28
00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.560
So they're going to take their time, at least at Google.

29
00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.000
We still kind of hand-review each PR that goes in.

30
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.160
We have like code reviews that are automated.

31
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.240
And we'll review the code, agent first, but then it still gets to a point

32
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.240
where a maintainer looks at the code and ships it

33
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.840
with all the security vulnerabilities and everything.

34
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.680
Right now, that has been at scale. We make sure that humans see everything.

35
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:50.040
So then they go and they post some feedback,

36
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:53.280
you know, hey, maybe you change these two things and

37
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:54.600
the drive-by

38
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.840
PR really is someone prompted it, but they had no expectation

39
00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.680
that they were actually going to have to follow up or do anything.

40
00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:00.960
Right.

41
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.760
So it might have been a good first judgment, being like, hey,

42
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.880
I can try to fix this, but they're not going to come back.

43
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.280
So you just have a prompt and forget it.

44
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.120
Where now it's either the maintainer needs to come and kind of take over that

45
00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.280
pull request, and it just kind of floats away into the abyss.

46
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.160
The second one is agents, like we showed in that quick example of ten subagents.

47
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.200
It's super easy to parallelize work.

48
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.960
Unfortunately, we have not figured out how to do that as humans

49
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:34.520
for our own brains, so we might be able to create code super, super fast.

50
00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:39.200
But the burden on the maintainer if we spin up ten pull requests in a minute.

51
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.680
It's not going to take 10 seconds to review all that code generated.

52
00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.160
So there's a disconnect there.

53
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.240
And trust is somewhat breaking down.

54
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.360
Like I mentioned, you don't necessarily know whether or not

55
00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.440
a human is on the other side of that code, whether it was even looked at.

56
00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.080
So when someone puts up a pull request, normally you would have gone back

57
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.480
and forth: hey, why did you make this design decision? Nowadays,

58
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.600
I don't know when I told me to or Claude told me to.

59
00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:13.120
So now the thing is, there's not actually trust there.

60
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:16.720
You can't actually assume that whoever put up that code even knows

61
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.160
what's going on.

62
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.240
We also heard from Dave Kerr of McKinsey,

63
00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.360
who came at this from an angle I didn't expect.

64
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.320
When one person generates a pile of code they don't understand,

65
00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:32.320
three things drift apart: their model of the system,

66
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.800
the system itself and their colleagues' model.

67
00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.040
Which is why he thinks review is not quality control.

68
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.040
It's how you engage with somebody else's mental model.

69
00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:45.160
Death by a thousand cuts.

70
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.880
I'm going to briefly talk about this because you probably know about it.

71
00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.000
Your attention just gets destroyed.

72
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.280
Short-cycle work that you're doing.

73
00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.200
Being able to work on everything kills your attention.

74
00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.720
We're constantly multitasking.

75
00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.840
I think most of us are feeling things like this.

76
00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.360
So how do you deal with it? Focus.

77
00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:06.680
Creating space for the work that you're doing.

78
00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.680
Running tasks to completion.

79
00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.880
Recognizing when you're engaging in displacement activities.

80
00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.880
Prioritizing. Creating protected time.

81
00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.600
Pressured speaking.

82
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.720
We'll get past this one,

83
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.800
except for the fact that your AI speaks to you like someone who's on cocaine.

84
00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:28.280
It's like nonstop, doesn't listen.

85
00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:30.720
No space for interruptions. That's pressured speaking.

86
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.320
It's awful to listen to.

87
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.400
What's even more awful is when a bunch of your colleagues

88
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:37.600
take all the pressured speaking from their AI

89
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.520
and throw it at you, and then you're like, God, it's coming from all directions.

90
00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.520
So what do we do about that? Compress signal to noise.

91
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.080
Like, what's the high-level message? What should this mean?

92
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.920
Not what's the 30 pages of stuff that is your stream of consciousness

93
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.920
that I don't necessarily want to see?

94
00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:54.480
So this is the key one.

95
00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.280
I'm interested in maladaptive creativity.

96
00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.280
So this is going to get a bit philosophical. Mental model.

97
00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:07.120
On the left is me building a system, my model of it. Mental model on the right.

98
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.160
In the middle is the system itself, similar but different.

99
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.160
Next mental model is the model my colleague has.

100
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.640
They're slightly different, but they're sort of roughly in sync.

101
00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:20.640
Because we talk a lot, we make small changes.

102
00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.840
We have to look at what we've written.

103
00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:26.440
Now, when AI does a lot of this for you and it does it very fast,

104
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:28.480
the mental models start to look like this.

105
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.360
One person can create this palace of stuff.

106
00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.600
They don't really necessarily know what's in it.

107
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.080
What they've created is very different to their mental model.

108
00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.760
The mental model from the person who is trying to understand

109
00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:43.000
it is even more different when people say to me, you don't need

110
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.000
to review pull requests, you don't need to review specs.

111
00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.040
That's, that's wrong.

112
00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:51.240
You do, because it's not about quality control.

113
00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:55.120
It's about engaging with the mental model so that they can see what someone

114
00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.840
has built and how it works.

115
00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.920
Because part of my job and your jobs is also

116
00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:03.400
to understand that system so that we can fix it and maintain it.

117
00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.440
This is the hardest thing to manage, but it's the thing that I think

118
00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.680
will help people the most.

119
00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.040
So you share, you collaborate, you build your models together.

120
00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:15.240
Brian Douglas, you may know as bdougie, is

121
00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.400
at Paper Compute, and was it GitHub before that?

122
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.440
He left an agent running for hours and came back to find it had achieved

123
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:26.120
almost nothing while cheerfully reporting progress.

124
00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.040
His phrase for it is politely hallucinating.

125
00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:32.720
So the agent was

126
00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.080
it wasn't learning, it was just politely hallucinating progress.

127
00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.120
It would actually celebrate wins of like, hey, we finished a thousand turns.

128
00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.120
We did it absolutely right.

129
00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:42.400
You guys know that from Claude.

130
00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.640
It would basically just do this and this is back in February.

131
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.880
So yeah, this would have been like 5 for Codex and like one of the Opuses.

132
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.960
But what I'm getting at is like this was like a nice construct

133
00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:58.240
for me to take that context and go serve it back to the agent.

134
00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.120
So a lot of folks we talked to upstairs, like,

135
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.280
they have a skill where they kind of sit and look at their sessions

136
00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:03.960
at the end of the week,

137
00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.480
and then they summarize, like stuff that's been done, good for you.

138
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.160
That's great.

139
00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.080
But you're definitely like top-notch when it comes to like using agents.

140
00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.800
But I don't do that.

141
00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.920
So instead I just dump all the context into a Tapes database.

142
00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:20.480
So at the time we were using SQLite,

143
00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.480
we've actually moved on to Postgres for limitation reasons.

144
00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.480
SQLite has single-player methodologies.

145
00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.560
Only one person can write to the database.

146
00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.400
This setup was actually ten Pokemon agents running simultaneously,

147
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.680
1,000 turns each, and learning. It's constantly piggybacking. So.

148
00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.840
So I have the Tapes database, which has all the raw data.

149
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.520
So this is the log for agents.

150
00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.880
And then I have this memory folder that I

151
00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:47.440
this is a concept I got from a Mastra blog post on observational memory.

152
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.160
And this is like taking your 1,000 sessions and then writing an observation.

153
00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:52.320
So think about the end of the day.

154
00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.400
You open up your journal like, man, I saw this amazing

155
00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.680
talk from Brian Douglas from San Francisco, California.

156
00:07:58.680 --> 00:07:59.640
It was amazing.

157
00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.320
He had a great shirt on, his glasses.

158
00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:03.040
I'm just kidding.

159
00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:04.360
I don't know if you did this the other day,

160
00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.000
but these are observations at the end of your sessions.

161
00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.480
I was just dropping these in markdown.

162
00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:13.000
So if these get written by the agent, the agent gets me human-readable feedback.

163
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:16.680
So things like when I found out, oh, the MCP has no idea.

164
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.280
We've not talked to any MCP.

165
00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:20.720
We have no context of the awareness of the entire world.

166
00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.360
We should build that into the into the loop.

167
00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:29.240
And to close, Dave Farley, who is having none of it.

168
00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.880
He puts natural language up against what a programming language

169
00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.880
actually gives you and finds it wanting.

170
00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:37.200
Worth saying.

171
00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.560
He writes no code by hand himself.

172
00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.320
This is not nostalgia.

173
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:45.360
How does natural language measure up to that?

174
00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.640
Because natural language, somebody said today, I think they quoted Andrej

175
00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:55.040
Karpathy saying that the programming language of the future is English.

176
00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.640
I don't like that idea very much for the reasons that I'm talking about.

177
00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.760
It's not precise enough, it's too vague.

178
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:05.800
Natural language helps us.

179
00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:06.800
How does that measure up?

180
00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.800
Helps us to organize our thinking about a problem?

181
00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:13.160
Well, it's too vague, really.

182
00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:16.600
Natural language is open to misinterpretation.

183
00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:17.960
If anybody's been around.

184
00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:19.560
I can't really see you very well.

185
00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:23.760
But if you've got a gray beard like mine and you've been around enough,

186
00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:30.080
I bet that you've been in a situation where somebody coming down from on high

187
00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:34.720
and given a very vague instruction to a bunch of programmers, has gone away

188
00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.880
and then been really disappointed in the results

189
00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:40.080
because they didn't tell us in enough detail, that's really common.

190
00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:44.560
It's really easy to misinterpret what the goal really is.

191
00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:49.400
Natural

192
00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.520
language is always open to interpretation.

193
00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:54.400
Time flies like an arrow.

194
00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:55.600
These are some time flies.

195
00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.600
Liking arrows.

196
00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:04.720
Vibe coding alone is simply not good enough.

197
00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:09.640
If we're just chatting with the computer to express our needs, that's not enough.

198
00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:10.880
We need tools

199
00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.440
that allow us to be more precise than that, to be more specific than that,

200
00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.680
to be better prompters of what it is that we want to achieve.

201
00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:23.480
That doesn't mean that we need to look at all the code,

202
00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:27.120
and are only allowed to do this in a programming language.

203
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:31.840
I'm in the category of, it's been a long time now since I've.

204
00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:37.600
So I've written any code by hand because my AI agent writes all the code,

205
00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:42.800
but I've been more precise than just natural language in some respects.

206
00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:47.400
In specifying what I want from it, I'm able to get what I want from it

207
00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.240
by using a precise,

208
00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:53.120
prescriptive version of natural language in order to be able to prompt it.

209
00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:56.080
And that's what I'm really talking about here.

210
00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.520
Thanks for watching.

211
00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.160
Be sure to join us for the next AI DevCon this November in New York City.

212
00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.120
Visit AI DevCon to learn more and book your ticket.
